WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:08.190
Every single second, your machine
processes massive streams of data.

2
00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:11.860
Without skipping a beat,
everything seems perfectly stable.

3
00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:15.260
But then, a software failure strikes.

4
00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:19.600
A simple disk driver tries to access
a memory address it shouldn't.

5
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:23.680
In some systems, this
single error cascades.

6
00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:27.760
The entire machine freezes and
your only option is a hard reboot.

7
00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:32.290
In other systems, that same faulty
driver is instantly isolated.

8
00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:36.850
It quietly restarts in the background, and
the machine keeps running as if nothing

9
00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:40.660
happened. The difference comes
down to the privilege boundary.

10
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:45.410
This is the strict line separating user
mode at the top from kernel mode at the

11
00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:50.550
bottom. Whether an operating system survives
a crash depends entirely on how much of

12
00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:54.160
its software is allowed to cross
that boundary into kernel mode.

13
00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:58.890
First, we have the monolithic
architecture, used by Linux and the BSDs.

14
00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:03.860
Here, every core service, the scheduler,
memory management, file systems, and

15
00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:06.490
drivers, is packed into kernel mode.

16
00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:09.190
This design is exceptionally fast.

17
00:01:09.410 --> 00:01:13.640
An application only needs one system
call, meaning just one crossing in and one

18
00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:18.800
crossing out. But because everything shares
kernel mode, a bug in a single driver

19
00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:22.650
can corrupt the shared memory space
and bring the entire system down.

20
00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:25.800
Linux is monolithic, but it is modular.

21
00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:31.480
This command output from the course
machine shows 258 loaded modules, which are

22
00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:34.250
drivers the system can
add or remove on the fly.

23
00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:40.450
Then we have the microkernel, used by
systems like seL4, QNX, and MINIX 3.

24
00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:46.150
This design moves most services, like file
systems and drivers, up into user mode.

25
00:01:46.430 --> 00:01:49.410
Only the bare minimum stays
below the privilege boundary.

26
00:01:49.570 --> 00:01:51.490
This provides strict isolation.

27
00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:54.880
If a driver fails in user mode,
the system simply restarts it.

28
00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:57.290
The kernel itself is never touched.

29
00:01:57.630 --> 00:01:59.250
The trade-off is performance.

30
00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:02.960
Operations now require many more messages
and boundary crossings to get the same

31
00:02:03.010 --> 00:02:08.190
work done. There is also a hybrid
approach, used by Windows NT and macOS.

32
00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:13.500
This is a compromise, keeping most services
in kernel mode, but moving some to user

33
00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:16.710
space, inheriting both
benefits and problems.

34
00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:21.540
Designing what runs in kernel mode is a
strict engineering choice, between raw

35
00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:24.410
execution speed and isolated resilience.

36
00:02:24.990 --> 00:02:30.200
So how exactly do these complex kernel
architectures actually load into memory from

37
00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:32.770
a completely powered off, dark state?

38
00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:35.050
It happens in four stages.

39
00:02:35.570 --> 00:02:37.880
First is the UEFI firmware.

40
00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:42.320
It tests the hardware, initializes the
memory and buses, and checks its non

41
00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:44.330
-volatile memory for boot instructions.

42
00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:48.380
Next is the bootloader,
like GRUB or systemd-boot.

43
00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:54.350
It takes the kernel, named vmlinuz, and
an initial file system called initramfs,

44
00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:55.930
and loads them into memory.

45
00:02:56.510 --> 00:03:01.200
Stage 3 is the kernel. It decompresses,
sets up virtual memory, detects the

46
00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:05.250
processors, and launches the
very first process, PID 1.

47
00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:11.300
Finally, user space. The systemd process,
running as PID 1, starts all the

48
00:03:11.350 --> 00:03:14.890
remaining services in parallel
until you reach the login prompt.

49
00:03:15.070 --> 00:03:17.440
Every other process descends
from this single node.

50
00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:22.000
We can measure exactly how long this
entire boot process takes on the course

51
00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:25.170
machine, using the
systemd-analyze command.

52
00:03:25.390 --> 00:03:27.330
This chart shows the time breakdown.

53
00:03:27.610 --> 00:03:33.340
The firmware took 9.1 seconds, the loader
took 4.9 seconds, and user space took 14.5

54
00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:37.570
seconds. The kernel stage
only took 2.6 seconds.

55
00:03:37.990 --> 00:03:42.210
Despite being the most complex and critical
component of the entire architecture,

56
00:03:42.490 --> 00:03:45.440
loading the kernel is actually the
fastest stage of the boot sequence.

57
00:03:45.490 --> 00:03:50.280
An operating system's resilience is defined
by its architecture, and bringing it to

58
00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:54.310
life requires a precise,
4-step sequential handoff.

59
00:03:54.950 --> 00:03:59.380
Let's review. In which kernel architecture
does a failing driver crash the whole

60
00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:02.470
system? The monolithic architecture.

61
00:04:03.110 --> 00:04:06.890
Question 2. Which boot stage is
the shortest on the course machine?

62
00:04:07.330 --> 00:04:14.850
The kernel. Now open animations A1.2 and
A1.3 in the course hub, and run lab TD0.
